 or Special Forces what I was doing  and I was  mostly in Africa I was doing armed deals  I was doing taking care of warlords I  was building small armies for US  interest in one case even helped prevent  a genocide in rwanda and burundi region  these are things that traditionally the  CIA would have done the reason why it's  being outsourced now is for a few  reasons one is that when you really want  something done with a zero footprint  operation you don't go to the CIA you  don't go to the Pentagon you could but  you can also go to the pen you also go  to the private sector because they give  you great plausible deniability meaning  that if something goes wrong if the  mission of two politically risky you  don't want your SEAL team guys plated on  television as prisoners of war then you  hire contractors because you could say  well we don't know who really they were  it gives you a new layer of plausible  deniability which matters a great deal  in an Information Age does that  plausible deniability come at a cost in  the sense that an operation carried out  by a group like the one you worked for  would be sort of apples to apples more  expensive than the government handling  it internally well actually contractors  are cheaper than the government and they  can actually be better than the  government at what they do we always  think that contractors are not good at  what they do they over charge yeah they  do overcharge but they can actually be  very effective however you know I think  that the bigger point is when you when  you outsource to companies you don't  really also know what you're getting is  a lot of problems with us most people  will think that you're getting like X  guys like myself US Army guys you're not  I was working alongside with people from  you know Salvador Philippines Mexico in  some ways the globalization of force is  like the globalization of a t-shirt it  comes to cheaper if it's coming from a  sort of so-called developing nation and  so 